{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546702054,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE2ODM5MzIzNQ=="},"target":"95a82190abb50ec486dc7cd17acbc5035ea9b851f750440c30a6889d210f1916","message":"Use case needed here: https://github.com/datalad/datalad-revolution/pull/71\n\nIdeally `git commit` should be able to exhibit its normal behavior, i.e. ask for a commit message when none was provided (by launching an editor), even when executed through DataLad's runner. However, when we run `git commit`, we also need to be able to act on a variety of error conditions, hence we need to capture stdout/err. ATM runner can capture the output streams XOR let them through. In shell lingo, we can do\n\n`git commit` and `git commit \u003e log`, but for the desired behavior we need `git commit | tee log`\n\nLooking through the Runner code it is not immediately clear to me where/if such feature could be added, but here is a sketch of the concept: https://shallowsky.com/blog/programming/python-tee.html","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1546728907,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTY5OTEwOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3103#issuecomment-451699109"},"message":"AFAIK \n- runner supports commands with prompts e.g. asking for credentials. But that is usually done via directly talking to /dev/tty, so it is presented to the user even from within datalad sandwiched within git annex remote call, which is done by datalad itself. We use getpass.getpass for that. see dialog.py\n- `| tee log` can also be done \n```\n log_stdout: bool or callable or 'online' or 'offline'\n```\nso if it is a callable -- you would get line as soon as it is received. That is what we use for e.g. capturing progress reports from git-annex\n\nI could be wrong, but in the context of `git commit`, to display an entire editor, we probably need to surrender any opportunity to receive stdout, and use smth like `os.system` or `pty.spawn` instead of popen..., e.g.\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'import os; os.system(\"vim 2\u003e/tmp/2\")'            \n$\u003e ls -ld /tmp/2\n-rw------- 1 yoh yoh 0 Jan  5 17:54 /tmp/2\n```\nbut again -- I could be wrong and missing what is needed","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546764990,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTcyNTk1NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3103#issuecomment-451725955"},"message":"It is perfectly OK to use `vim` through Runner.\n\n```python\nfrom datalad.cmd import Runner\nRunner().run('vim', log_stdout=False, log_stderr=False)\n```\n\nOTOH using a callable to intercept and pass on anything that comes through the pipe does not work.\n\n```python\nRunner().run('vim', log_stdout=lambda x: sys.stdout.write(x), log_stderr=lambda x: sys.sterr.write(x))\n```\n\nThe reason for that is still unclear to me. I suspect that it never gets to see \"a line\".","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1546867026,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTkzMTUzMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3103#issuecomment-451931531"},"message":"FWIW:\nJust tried and it seems to me, that - at least for this use case - it works if only stderr is logged. Meaning\n\n```python\nfrom datalad.cmd import Runner\nRunner('.').run('git commit', log_stdout=False, log_stderr=True)\n```\nWill launch `vim`.\nNot sure though whether stderr is all we currently need.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1546868297,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ1MTkzNzIwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3103#issuecomment-451937202"},"message":"That is true, but when I tried this, it could not act on any error, as Runner always returned `''` as stderr.","files":null}]}